{
 "name": "hypoxemia overview",
 "width": 1544,
 "title": "Hypoxemia worked out from the arterial blood gas: a normal alveolar-arterial gradient means too little oxygen reaches the alveoli (low inspired oxygen at altitude, or hypoventilation with a high PaCO2); a raised gradient means oxygen fails to cross into blood, sorted by diffusion capacity and imaging (diffusion limitation), the response to 100% oxygen (shunt) or neither (ventilation-perfusion mismatch, the commonest); a normal PaO2 with hypoxia suggests carbon monoxide",
